  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Damp and dreary conditions continue through \nWednesday afternoon with drier weather returning Thursday.\n\nNot a whole lot has changed in the synoptic pattern with \nstrong/broad wedging high pressure over southeast Quebec and a \nstalled boundary over the VA/NC border. Ripples of low pressure \ncontinue to traverse this boundary to the south sending on and off \nshowers to the region.\n\nExpect a steady supply of abundant low cloud cover, showers/drizzle, \nand cool temperatures to continue through at least Wednesday \nafternoon. This is due largely in part to a series of low pressure \nsystems passing along the stalled front to the south and a delay of \nmid level dry air from the north as wedging high pressure \nstrengthens over southeast Canada. Conditions will slowly improve \nfrom north to south late Wednesday morning into Wednesday evening.\n\nThe wedging high pressure system should allow for the drainage of \nmore dry air to the south shunting the front into the Carolinas \nwhile also developing a coastal low pressure system off the \nDelmarva/NC coast. This coastal low feature will supply extra \nsubsidence Wednesday night into Thursday allowing for widespread \ndrying conditions across the region. Unfortunately, with the low \nnearby breezy conditions will remain along with coastal flooding \nwoes over sensitive locations within the bay/tidal Potomac (see \ntides discussion).\n\nGiven increased northeast flow expect steady temperatures in the \nupper 50s and mid 60s to continue through Wednesday with a warming \ntrend Thursday as some sunshine returns. Northeasterly winds will \ngusts 15-30 mph at times with the highest gusts over the bay and \nhigher ridges, especially Wednesday through Friday as coastal low \npressure strengthens. The wind will give us more of a raw late Fall \nfeel heading into Wednesday afternoon. Highs get back into the low \nto mid 60s with even few spots hitting 70 Thursday afternoon. \n\nRainfall wise most locations should see 1-3 inches of rainfall \n(includes what has fallen since Monday, and what is yet to fall \nthrough Wednesday afternoon). This should be beneficial in nature \ngiven the ongoing drought conditions.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Turning breezy and drier with a gradual warming \ntrend Friday into the upcoming weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will start weakening on Thu as low pressure forms a \ncouple hundred miles off the coast and begins to track north and \nthen west toward the coast. Global models show the low pressure \nmaking its closest approach to our area Sunday afternoon. Ensemble \nguidance show most of the rain staying to our east, but there is a \nsmall chance that it could make it as far west as I-95. The \nnortheast flow should start weakening Thu, but there is still a \nchance that the winds may strengthen again Fri night into the \nweekend as low pressure approaches the coast. At this time, there \nisn't a definite clear trend of what winds will do this weekend as \nthe track of the low and how close it gets to the coast remains \nhighly uncertain.",
